# Line endings: LF everywhere, in the repository and in the working tree.
#
# This project is developed from Linux and Windows against the *same* checkout
# on a shared partition — one working tree, one .git, two operating systems.
# That rules out the usual "LF in the repository, native in the working tree"
# arrangement: there is only one working tree, so whichever side checked out
# last would decide what the other side sees, and `core.autocrlf` cannot help.
# Hence `eol=lf` rather than plain `text=auto`.
#
# Before this file existed, ten source files were CRLF (src/color.rs,
# pipeline.rs, types.rs, chroma.rs, metrics.rs, reference.rs, sharpen.rs,
# whitebalance.rs, redecode.rs, tools/contact-sheet.py) purely because they were
# last saved from the Windows side, and any tool that rewrote one of them from
# Linux turned a four-line edit into a whole-file diff. Nothing in the build
# cares which ending it gets; the diffs did.
#
# `build-release.bat` is deliberately not forced to CRLF. It is LF today and
# builds on Windows, and cmd.exe only trips over LF-only batch files in
# constructs this one does not use.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, never converted or diffed as text. Git's own detection already gets
# these right; the point of listing them is that the rule stops depending on
# heuristics.
*.pdf binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
